# WimForge -- what never belongs in the repository.
#
# Two things drive this list, and neither is obvious from a clean checkout:
#
#   1. Setup can now put the workspace NEXT TO THE TOOLKIT. That is offered with
#      a warning when the toolkit is in a working tree, but somebody will
#      eventually take it -- and then images, an expanded driver library and a
#      live unattend.xml are all sitting inside the repository. Everything the
#      workspace generates is ignored by name, whether or not it exists here yet.
#
#   2. Several of the files this toolkit handles carry credentials. An
#      unattend.xml holds a local administrator password; a provisioning package
#      can hold a domain-join account and Wi-Fi keys; a .pfx holds a private key.
#      Those come first, because a large binary committed by accident is an
#      annoyance and a password committed by accident is an incident.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- credentials

# An unattend.xml contains <AdministratorPassword> and usually the domain join.
# The one under ReferenceBuild is the documented TEMPLATE -- placeholder where
# the password goes -- and belongs in the repo, so it is let back in explicitly.
# Any other unattend.xml is a live one.
#
# Matched loosely on purpose. 'unattend.xml' alone would let through
# 'live-unattend.xml', 'unattend-pos.xml' and every other name somebody reaches
# for when they keep a second copy -- and a second copy is exactly the one with
# the real password in it.
*unattend*.xml
!ReferenceBuild/unattend.xml

# Provisioning packages. The ones in \Recovery\Customizations are re-applied
# after a reset, and a package that joins a domain or configures Wi-Fi carries
# the credentials to do it.
*.ppkg

# Private keys and certificate stores.
*.pfx
*.p12
*.pem
*.key

# The live configuration is per-machine: local drive letters, share paths, VM and
# host names. Created on first run and kept in %ProgramData%; only the example
# belongs in the repo, and it is named differently so it survives this.
WimForge.config.json
config.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------- images and payload

# Multi-GB binaries have no business in git. Version the manifests that describe
# them instead -- the build history records what went into each one.
*.wim
*.esd
*.swm
*.vhd
*.vhdx
*.iso
*.msu
*.cab
*.working.wim

# Part of the recovery payload, and not covered by anything above.
boot.sdi

# Harvested driver packages. Export-WfModelDriver pulls whole DriverStore
# packages off a reference machine; there is no hand-written binary in this
# repository, so anything matching these is harvested output.
*.sys
*.cat
*.dll
*.inf
*.din

# A compiled local policy blob: binary, machine-specific, and replaced wholesale
# rather than merged.
Registry.pol

# ------------------------------------------------------------- workspace trees
#
# Only created here if somebody points the workspace at the toolkit folder. Named
# anyway, because the moment to have ignored them is before that happens.

Workspace/
Imaging/
Images/
Drivers/
Updates/
Payload/
Export/
Logs/
Recovery/

# Mount points and scratch. A mounted image inside a working tree makes every git
# status enumerate a full Windows installation -- Test-WfMountPath warns about
# exactly this.
Mount/
Mounts/
Scratch/
WimMount/
WimScratch/
*-Pe/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- output

build-history.json
*.log

# Superseded work moved aside rather than deleted: the remote session cannot
# remove files on a connected folder, so it lands here to be deleted by hand.
_to_delete/

# --------------------------------------------------------- editor and OS noise

.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.suo
*.user
*.swp
*.orig
*.rej
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
